lesson: 76
title: How many more, how many fewer
stars: 0
explain: To compare two numbers, we ask how many more one has than the other.
explain: Match them up one to one; the ones without a partner are the difference, and we find it by subtracting the smaller from the bigger.
explain: Anna has 14 shells and Sam has 9: 14 - 9 = 5, so Anna has 5 more shells than Sam, and Sam has 5 fewer than Anna.
vars: a=name b=name t=thing n=5..90 m=2..80
need: n > m
answer: n - m
exercise: {a} has {n} {t}. {b} has {m} {t}. How many more {t} does {a} have than {b}?
exercise: {a} has {n} {t} and {b} has {m} {t}. How many fewer {t} does {b} have than {a}?
exercise: {b} has {m} {t}. {a} has {n} {t}. How many more {t} does {b} need to have as many as {a}?
exercise: There are {n} {t} in the red box and {m} {t} in the blue box. How many more {t} are in the red box?
exercise: {a} found {n} {t}. {b} found {m} {t}. How many fewer {t} did {b} find?
test: {b} owns {m} {t}, while {a} owns {n}. By how many {t} does {a} have the bigger pile?
test: What is the difference between {a}'s {n} {t} and {b}'s {m} {t}?
test: {b} wants exactly as many {t} as {a}. {a} has {n}; {b} has {m}. How many more must {b} get?
test: {b} has {m} {t} and {a} has {n}. How many {t} would {b} have to be given to catch up with {a}?
exercise: {a} collected {n} {t} and {b} collected {m} {t}. How many more {t} did {a} collect?
exercise: How many more {t} does {a} have than {b}, if {a} has {n} and {b} has {m}?
exercise: In Texas, {a} picked {n} {t}. {b} picked only {m}. How many fewer {t} did {b} pick than {a}?
exercise: {b} has {m} {t}. {a} has {n} {t}. How many {t} less does {b} have than {a}?
exercise: {b} has {m} {t}, not as many as {a}, who has {n}. How many fewer {t} does {b} have?
exercise: A shop in Manchester sold {n} {t} on Monday and {m} {t} on Tuesday. How many more {t} did it sell on Monday?
exercise: {a} scored {n} points in a game in Melbourne and {b} scored {m} points. By how many points did {a} win?
exercise: There are {n} {t} on the top shelf and {m} {t} on the bottom shelf. How many more {t} are on the top shelf than on the bottom one?
test: {a}'s class in Cork made {n} paper cranes; {b}'s class made {m}. How many cranes separate the two classes?
test: How many {t} must be added to {m} {t} to make {n} {t}?
test: {n} children live on one street in Wellington and {m} on the next. How many more children live on the first street?
test: {b} has {m.words} {t}. {a} has {n} {t}. Find how many fewer {t} {b} has.
test: A train in Scotland carries {n} passengers and a bus carries {m}. How many more passengers ride on the train?
test: At sports day in Perth, {a} jumped {n} cm and {b} jumped {m} cm. How many cm further did {a} jump?
exercise: {a}'s got {n} {t}, {b} only {m}. how many more {a} got
exercise: {b} has {m+3} {t} — no hang on, {m} — n {a} has {n}. how many fewer {b} got
exercise: {a} found {n} {t} at the beach, got sunburnt n all, {b} found {m}. how many more did {a} find
exercise: {b} wants as many {t} as {a}. {a} {n}, {b} {m}. me brother says {b} needs {n+m} more lol. nah, how many really
test: {n} vs {m} {t}, {a} and {b}. by how many is {a} ahead then
test: how many {t} {b} gotta get to catch {a}? {a} has {n}, {b} got {m-1}, sorry {m}
test: there were 3 teachers on the bus, anyway {a} picked {n} {t} n {b} {m}, whats the difference
exam: Whereas {a} holds {n} {t} and {b} holds {m}, determine the excess of {a}'s holding over that of {b}.
exam: Ascertain by how many {t} the holding of {b}, namely {m}, falls short of the holding of {a}, namely {n}.
exam: Compute the difference between {n.words} and {m.words}.
exam: It is given that {n} {t} are attributed to {a} and {m} {t} to {b}. State the number by which the former exceeds the latter.
exercise: Before Dad was let go, the family spent {n} dollars a week on food. Now they spend {m}. How many fewer dollars do they spend a week?
exercise: {a}'s report says {a.he} 'could do better': {a.he} got {m} spellings right, and {b} got {n}. How many more did {b} get right than {a}?
test: {b}'s 'bending the truth' again, says {b}'s got more {t} than {a}. bollocks — {a}'s got {n}, {b}'s got {m}. how many more's {a} got
